 # This class is part of an EXPERIMENTAL API.
class RetentionService:
    """RetentionService keeps a tenant's rules of how long its data must be kept
    and when it is due to go, lists what is due, disposes of it, and keeps
    holds that stop any of it from going. A rule does what it says; whether it
    meets a law or a consent form is the institution's to judge, and the
    server makes no claim of it.

    Rules. A rule has a name, a selector (the whole tenant, or the receiver,
    a route of it, or the primary storage a session's scans were archived
    through), a keep_at_least period and a dispose_after period, either or
    both, and an action: review (list the due sessions for a person) or
    delete. A receiver, route or storage removed later keeps matching the
    scans archived through it; a replica is never a selector's storage,
    since nothing is archived to it first. There is no grouping above the
    subject: a platform with its own collections places holds and deletes by
    subject through the API.

    Which sessions a rule matches. A session's scans may have arrived
    through different receivers, routes or storages. The gentler reading
    wins: a session matches a rule's keep_at_least when any of its scans
    arrived through the rule's selector, and a rule's dispose_after only
    when all of them did. A whole-tenant rule matches every session.

    Start time. A period counts from a session's start time: when the session
    was first archived, by the server's clock, never a DICOM date, shifted or
    not; or the retention_start an admin gave the import job that archived
    it (ImportJob), audited, and then the session is never due before the
    time it was archived plus the notice below. A file's header never sets
    it. A scan that arrives later joins its session's start time, and the
    session's due and keep times are stored again with it.

    When a session is due. Of the rules that match a session, the latest
    keep_at_least and the earliest dispose_after apply: the session is due
    once its start time plus that dispose_after has passed and no matching
    rule still keeps it. A session no rule's dispose_after matches is never
    due. Its rule is the one with the earliest dispose_after (by name on a
    tie), and its action is delete only when every rule whose dispose_after
    matches it says delete. Due and keep times are stored per session when
    it is archived, when a scan joins it and when a rule changes, so a
    listing reads them and computes nothing.

    Changes, and the notice. Every change of a rule (adding, changing,
    removing) applies at once, and the due and keep times it stores are
    clamped: a change that would make a session due sooner, or keep it for
    less, makes it due no sooner, and keeps it at least until, the change's
    time plus the tenant's setting retention.shorten-notice (a period such as
    "30d", from "1d" to "365d"; set on a tenant by the installation's admin
    only, with an installation value, the default for every tenant; built-in
    default "30d"), and never later than the times before the change. So a
    mistyped rule cannot make a year's data due tonight, and a change that
    keeps data longer takes effect at once. A removal is not undone during
    the notice: the clamp keeps what it would have dropped until the notice
    ends, and adding the rule again keeps it at once.

    The clock. A daily pass per tenant computes what has become due and, for
    delete rules, queues the disposal. It keeps the time of its last pass.
    When the server's clock has moved forward by more than the installation's
    setting retention.max-clock-step since then (a period such as "2d", from
    "1h" to "30d"; installation-only, since the clock is the server's;
    built-in default "2d"), the tenant's retention is paused with the reason
    "clock jumped", an EVENT_TYPE_RETENTION_CLOCK event is made, and, until
    an admin confirms the time (ConfirmClock), nothing becomes due, no
    dispose job of the pass runs, and no purge starts because its
    purge_time came (CorrectionService): only a purge a person asks for
    (PurgeCorrection) goes on. The same check runs before any purge starts
    by its purge_time, and at least every hour, not only at the pass. A
    clock moved back only delays, the safe direction. No time source outside
    the server is asked: that would send data out.

    Disposal. The due sessions of a rule are deleted by a dispose job
    (DisposeJob): the daily pass queues one for each delete rule with due
    sessions and no dispose job queued or running, and an admin's Dispose
    queues one from the list the admin saw. Each session is deleted through
    CorrectionService's ordinary delete with origin retention and the rule's
    name; a delete an admin's Dispose made carries that admin's key and
    reason, and one the daily pass made carries no key and the reason the
    server writes, "retention rule <rule>, due <date>". It is then a delete
    in every way (records, publish, grace, undo, purge on every copy,
    arriving again). A person who undoes a retention delete exempts its
    session from that rule's daily pass (DueSession.undone) until a person
    disposes of it with Dispose or the rule changes: the pass never deletes
    again what a person brought back. A hold is the other way to keep it,
    with a reason, against every rule. At most the tenant's setting
    retention.max-per-pass sessions go per job (a whole number from 1 to
    100000; set on a tenant by the installation's admin only, with an
    installation value; built-in default "1000"), the oldest due first. A
    held session is never disposed of, and no DICOM date changes when
    anything goes.

    Holds. A hold is a mark, with a reason, on a subject, a session, a scan
    or the whole tenant, placed and released by an admin. It has no expiry:
    it lasts until a person releases it. It covers what it names now and
    what arrives under it later, and follows a relabel. While it holds, a
    delete of what it covers is refused (precondition, reason HELD), a purge
    of it waits with the state reason "held" before its next object, and
    disposal skips it; a delete committed before it stays committed and may
    still be undone. A replace or a relabel under a hold is allowed, since
    nothing is lost, and the purge of what it superseded waits; a relabel
    rewrites the hold records of what it moves before its publish.

    Hold records. Before a hold's row commits as active, a hold record,
    meta/.holds/<hold id>.json, is put on the primary of everything the
    target touches; each replica that answers gets it too, and one that does
    not is owed it (ReplicaService: no purge removes anything from it, and
    no switch to it swaps, until it has the record; Hold.storages_pending
    names it). A release rewrites the records likewise. A record found
    active with no row, by a rebuild, a restore, or the server's own start,
    is made an active hold (Hold.found_on_storage), since keeping data
    longer is the lesser error: a restore from a backup older than a hold
    still keeps what the hold protects. A storage's object lock is reported
    as a purge's retained disposition (CorrectionService), never as a hold.

    Who sees a hold. That a hold exists, its id and what it covers are
    shown wherever it stops something: a delete's refusal and preview, a due
    session marked held, the EVENT_TYPE_HOLD_CHANGED event. Its reason, a
    person's words about a subject, is answered only to an operator or more
    (GetHold, ListHolds).

    Every method acts inside one tenant, named by the request's tenant field
    (ConfirmClock by its scope, which may be the installation), so a request
    into a tenant that is not ready is answered UNAVAILABLE. No answer of
    this service speaks of another tenant.
    """

